<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyramid of the Sun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pyramid of the Sun is the largest building at Teotihuacan and one of the largest pyramids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, about 65 metres high, raised on the east side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avenue-of-the-dead">Avenue of the Dead</a> and probably completed by about 200 CE. A man-made cave runs beneath it. Its present form owes much to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopoldo-batres">Leopoldo Batres</a>'s reconstruction, completed in 1910.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun.jpg" alt="Pyramid of the Sun" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Burkhard Mücke · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2020-02-11_Teotihuacan_la_Avenida_de_los_Muertos_y_la_Pir%C3%A1mide_del_Sol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
